0:{"rsc":["$","$1","c",{"children":[[["$","$L2","5.3",{"topic":{"id":"5.3","title":"Threat Hunting and Threat Analysis","minutes":14,"summary":"Hunting proactively tests whether suspicious behavior is present, using hypotheses, baselines, and evidence rather than waiting for an alert.","sections":[{"title":"Hypothesis-driven investigation","body":["A hunt begins with a testable proposition, such as whether a suspicious authentication pattern indicates token misuse in a defined environment. Identify the expected observations, required telemetry, normal baseline, and conditions that would disprove the hypothesis. A vague instruction to “find hackers” does not provide a useful scope.","Hunting differs from alert triage and incident response, although they overlap. Triage evaluates an existing alert; hunting searches for behavior that may not have triggered one; response contains and recovers from an identified incident. Define the activity and authority so a hunt does not become an uncontrolled intrusive test."]},{"title":"Behavior, indicators, and analytical models","body":["The Pyramid of Pain illustrates that some adversary behaviors are harder to change than simple hashes or IP addresses. It is a conceptual model, not a numerical law. Focus on reliable behaviors and contextual combinations rather than treating every technique match as malicious. ATT&CK can help express what was observed and where visibility is missing.","Use baselines for accounts, processes, destinations, and administrative actions. Look for sequences: unusual login, new token, changed mailbox rule, and data access. A single event may be benign; a corroborated sequence may justify escalation. Record the query, dataset, time range, and false-positive considerations."]},{"title":"From hunt to validated outcome","body":["A hunt can find malicious activity, benign anomalies, or visibility gaps. All three can be useful. Validate important findings before containment that could disrupt legitimate services. Preserve the relevant records and hand off to incident response with a clear account of what is known and unknown.","Measure coverage, repeatability, validation quality, and decision impact rather than only counting alerts. Convert a successful hunt into a tested detection or improved control where appropriate. Document why a hypothesis was rejected and what data would improve the next hunt. Avoid retrospective claims that the hunt proved every absent threat."]}],"practice":{"title":"Design a token-misuse hunt","scenario":"A fictional tenant has sign-in logs, token events, and mailbox-rule changes.","steps":["Write a hypothesis and an innocent alternative.","Define the accounts, time window, and required fields.","Correlate events and record missing telemetry.","Classify the outcome as confirmed, benign, or unresolved with next steps."],"debrief":"A good hunt produces a defensible answer or a clearly identified visibility gap—not a predetermined accusation."},"terms":[],"lawIds":["it-act","bnss"],"sources":["mitre","first","nist-ir"],"labId":"network","unit":5,"objectives":["Explain hypothesis-driven investigation and apply it to an investigation.","Explain behavior, indicators, and analytical models and apply it to an investigation.","Explain from hunt to validated outcome and apply it to an investigation."],"question":{"id":"q-5.3","prompt":"What is a valid outcome of a threat hunt?","options":["Only a confirmed attacker.","A benign explanation or a documented visibility gap.","A guaranteed conviction.","An untested detection deployed permanently."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Hunting tests hypotheses. Disproved hypotheses and missing telemetry are useful results when documented.","unit":5}}}]],["$L3"],"$L4"]}],"isPartial":false,"staleTime":300,"varyParams":null,"buildId":"mMiCr23A_-Cpk0hZkfT7X"}
